{
  "description": "Each case starts an empty act for its input's hello, applies ops in order and expects act, the act the core writes for tick. An op sets a control field ({set, arg}), reads one back ({get}, giving value or none), asks to fire ({fire}) or adds raw top-level fields ({extra}, a JSON object); error gives a refusal's status. A later write replaces a field and the others keep theirs; a refused write leaves the field as it was; get gives the value as set, before clamping. The act holds only the fields set, clamped: drive fields to their schema limits, headings by normalize, pitches to robot.maxPitch, the scan arc to scanner.minArc to maxArc, the power to gun.power.min to max, the status value to 1e9 and its text to 32 code points. fire is sent only after a fire op, with the power if one was set. Members come in the order v, type, tick, drive, forces, aim, scan, fire, status, then the extra fields in the order first given, and each group's fields in schema order; compare the key order too.",
  "encoding": "A case gives the call, its args (none for a property) and one outcome: value (CY_OK and the result), ok: true (CY_OK from a call with no result), none: true (CY_NONE, absent) or error (the status name, such as E_RANGE). A stream's configure gives the args of tracker.configure, called after the hello and before its cases. Records use the canonical snake_case field names. A number argument written {\"f64\": \"NaN\"} is that non-finite double (NaN, Infinity, -Infinity or -0). slots lists the id of each robot slot after the state: slot n is slots[n - 1], and you are slot 1.",
  "tolerance": 0,
  "rule": "|actual - expected| <= tolerance * max(1, |expected|), field by field; a case's tolerance is the file's unless tolerances names its call",
